WEBVTT

00:00.200 --> 00:03.616
Most project teams estimate in one tool, schedule in another,

00:03.616 --> 00:05.688
plan the field work in a spreadsheet,

00:05.688 --> 00:07.928
and track progress somewhere else again.

00:07.928 --> 00:10.256
Every one of those steps gets retyped.

00:10.776 --> 00:13.172
VRSmartPlan, formerly VRProject,

00:13.172 --> 00:16.891
brings thirteen tools together on one tool bar.

00:16.911 --> 00:18.505
VREstimator.

00:18.525 --> 00:22.322
The VRScheduler Schedule Editor and schedule analysis.

00:22.342 --> 00:25.175
VRPlanner and seven specialist planners.

00:25.195 --> 00:29.013
VRTAR for turnarounds, VRCMMS for maintenance,

00:29.013 --> 00:33.695
and VRControls for project controls and cost tracking.

00:34.615 --> 00:38.354
In this walkthrough, we'll follow the work the way a project does.

00:38.354 --> 00:41.470
Estimate it, schedule it, plan it, and then control it.

00:41.470 --> 00:44.390
Every project you'll see is synthetic demo data.

00:45.310 --> 00:46.966
We'll start with the estimate.

00:48.286 --> 00:51.848
VREstimator opens on a dashboard for the open estimate:

00:51.848 --> 00:53.921
total installed cost, man-hours,

00:53.921 --> 00:56.842
and the breakdown by discipline and craft.

00:57.462 --> 01:01.132
On the Takeoff Board, quantities are measured straight off the drawing.

01:01.132 --> 01:04.329
Count, length and area, after you set the drawing's scale.

01:04.949 --> 01:07.410
Every measurement becomes a takeoff line.

01:07.410 --> 01:11.491
Each line carries its quantity, its library man-hours and its craft,

01:11.491 --> 01:14.631
with discipline, global and item factors applied.

01:15.251 --> 01:18.391
Crews are built from crafts and equipment with their rates,

01:18.391 --> 01:21.571
so the labor and equipment behind every line is visible.

01:22.191 --> 01:24.726
The cost breakdown rolls labor, material,

01:24.726 --> 01:28.202
equipment and subcontract up to total installed cost.

01:28.822 --> 01:33.055
And on the Schedule tab, takeoff lines become activities. Crew size,

01:33.055 --> 01:36.416
crews in parallel and shift hours drive the durations,

01:36.416 --> 01:39.231
and labor and equipment load as resources.

01:39.851 --> 01:42.098
The proposal carries the estimate class,

01:42.098 --> 01:43.952
accuracy range and pricing basis,

01:43.952 --> 01:48.365
and an estimate can't be marked issued while quality checks are still open.

01:48.985 --> 01:50.430
Next, the schedule.

01:51.750 --> 01:55.613
The Schedule Editor puts the grid and the bar chart side by side.

01:55.613 --> 01:59.594
Type durations and predecessors, and the forward and backward pass,

01:59.594 --> 02:02.587
total float and critical path update as you go.

02:03.207 --> 02:06.897
The critical path view lists the driving activities with their total

02:06.897 --> 02:10.190
float, so you can see exactly what moves the finish date.

02:10.810 --> 02:15.166
The DCMA 14-point assessment shows which checks pass and which fail,

02:15.166 --> 02:17.160
with thresholds you can set.

02:17.780 --> 02:21.621
The S-curve plots planned, earned and actual on one dated axis,

02:21.621 --> 02:24.564
with staffing shown as full-time equivalents.

02:25.184 --> 02:29.509
And Monte Carlo risk runs duration ranges through the network to give a

02:29.509 --> 02:33.851
range for the finish date and the cost, explained in plain language.

02:33.871 --> 02:37.863
Earned value, baselines and version compare sit on the same tab bar.

02:38.483 --> 02:40.061
Now, the field plan.

02:41.381 --> 02:45.388
VRPlanner plans the work of a capital project before it's loaded into

02:45.388 --> 02:48.350
the schedule. The project view shows the man-hours,

02:48.350 --> 02:50.873
the manpower curve and the cost make-up.

02:51.493 --> 02:55.803
Each work item is stepped out from a module that asks its own questions.

02:55.803 --> 03:00.113
Civil, structural steel, piping, equipment, electrical, instrumentation,

03:00.113 --> 03:02.767
coatings and insulation, and completions.

03:03.387 --> 03:06.596
The answers give the work steps, with the crew size by craft,

03:06.596 --> 03:09.742
the equipment, the hours and the materials for each one.

03:10.362 --> 03:13.636
Work items are sequenced on the project's working calendar,

03:13.636 --> 03:16.245
and the manpower and equipment loading follows,

03:16.245 --> 03:17.999
by working day and by craft.

03:18.619 --> 03:21.757
The Preparation tab runs a gated preparation plan,

03:21.757 --> 03:24.644
with complexity and readiness scoring, alerts,

03:24.644 --> 03:27.041
tie-ins and preparation team hours.

03:27.661 --> 03:31.095
And progress is earned step by step, by rules of credit,

03:31.095 --> 03:33.073
not typed in as a percentage.

03:33.693 --> 03:36.422
VRPlanner has seven specialist siblings.

03:37.742 --> 03:41.573
A data center, a highway and a power outage don't plan the same way.

03:41.573 --> 03:44.955
So each specialist planner starts from its own work library,

03:44.955 --> 03:47.409
but all eight share one planning engine.

03:48.329 --> 03:52.074
VRDCPlanner covers data center campuses, including cooling,

03:52.074 --> 03:56.654
hall fit-out, rack integration, structured cabling and commissioning.

03:57.274 --> 04:01.972
VRE&IPlanner breaks electrical and instrumentation work into work steps,

04:01.972 --> 04:04.325
each with its crew and man-hours.

04:04.945 --> 04:07.930
VRPowerPlanner covers power plant construction,

04:07.930 --> 04:10.660
and VRPowerOutage covers the outage itself,

04:10.660 --> 04:13.401
with progress earned by rules of credit.

04:14.021 --> 04:17.335
VRUpstreamPlanner covers onshore field development,

04:17.335 --> 04:20.979
and VRMidstreamPlanner covers pipelines and stations.

04:21.599 --> 04:23.836
And the newest, VRRailHighwayPlanner,

04:23.836 --> 04:27.462
covers rail and heavy highway work, with the same man-hours,

04:27.462 --> 04:30.865
manpower curve and cost make-up from its own library.

04:31.485 --> 04:33.206
Here's where the tools connect.

04:34.526 --> 04:38.717
A plan from any of the eight planners goes into the Schedule Editor as

04:38.717 --> 04:42.727
activities with logic and resources, under the WBS node you choose,

04:42.727 --> 04:44.663
with one undo to take it out.

04:44.683 --> 04:49.420
Or it opens as a new schedule, or writes a file for Primavera P6.

04:50.140 --> 04:50.861
From there,

04:50.861 --> 04:54.989
the Schedule Editor sends the schedule to VRControls to set the

04:54.989 --> 04:58.461
baseline, then each statused update to report earned,

04:58.461 --> 05:01.085
actual and budget hours, SPI and CPI.

05:01.805 --> 05:06.644
VRControls opens on project health. Budget, earned and actual hours,

05:06.644 --> 05:11.184
with SPI and CPI worked on hours, and the curves behind them.

05:11.804 --> 05:14.504
Progress and earned value are tracked by activity,

05:14.504 --> 05:17.404
with shift-by-shift progress entry from the field.

05:18.024 --> 05:21.869
And completions sit in the same record, with systems and subsystems,

05:21.869 --> 05:24.387
the tag register, check sheets and punch.

05:25.007 --> 05:27.334
Two more tools round out the tool bar.

05:28.654 --> 05:32.862
VRTAR estimates turnaround scope one item at a time from a module

05:32.862 --> 05:33.380
library,

05:33.380 --> 05:37.853
and the whole event re-prices when you change a rate or an answer.

05:38.473 --> 05:43.113
And VRCMMS runs maintenance, with work orders, preventive maintenance,

05:43.113 --> 05:46.494
planning and backlog, inventory and reliability.

05:47.114 --> 05:48.490
Last, how you run it.

05:49.810 --> 05:52.984
VRSmartPlan runs the same online in the browser,

05:52.984 --> 05:55.962
or installed on a Windows or Mac computer.

05:55.982 --> 05:58.594
Installed, it runs from the installed files,

05:58.594 --> 06:02.771
with no internet connection, no server and no administrator rights.

06:02.791 --> 06:06.130
And your records stay on the computer, in a private profile.

06:07.050 --> 06:10.819
Thirteen tools. One tool bar. That's VRSmartPlan.

06:11.219 --> 06:13.055
Request a demonstration.
